Presidential elections were held in [[Colombia]] on 27 May 2018.<ref>[http://www.registraduria.gov.co/IMG/pdf/Resolucion_5552_del_26_de_mayo_de_2017_1.pdf Resolution No 5552] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606083604/http://registraduria.gov.co/IMG/pdf/Resolucion_5552_del_26_de_mayo_de_2017_1.pdf |date=2017-06-06 }} [[National Civil Registry (Colombia)]]</ref> As no candidate received a majority of the vote, the second round of voting was held on 17 June.<ref name=ASCOA>{{cite news |last1=Sonneland |first1=Holly |title=Explainer: Colombia's 2018 Elections |url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-colombias-2018-elections |access-date=3 September 2022 |work=As/Coa |date=28 June 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220815030829/https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-colombias-2018-elections |archive-date=15 August 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> Incumbent president Juan Manuel Santos was ineligible to seek a third term. [[Iván Duque]], a senator, defeated [[Gustavo Petro]], former [[Superior Mayor of Bogotá|mayor of Bogotá]], in the second round. Duque's victory made him one of the youngest individuals elected to the presidency, aged 42. His running mate, [[Marta Lucía Ramírez]], was the first woman elected to the vice presidency in Colombian history.<ref name=duque>{{cite news |last1=Casey |first1=Nicholas |last2=Abad |first2=Susan |title=Colombia Elects Iván Duque, Populist Conservative, as President |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/17/world/americas/colombia-election-ivan-duque.html |access-date=8 September 2022 |work=New York Times |date=17 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618015359/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/17/world/americas/colombia-election-ivan-duque.html |archive-date=18 June 2018|url-status=live|url-access=subscription |location=Bogotá}}</ref>

The election was held following a peace agreement between the government and the [[Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia]] (FARC) in 2016, ending over 50 years of conflict.<ref name="jpd">{{cite news |author1=Joe Parkin Daniels |title=Iván Duque wins election to become Colombia's president |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/jun/18/ivan-duque-wins-election-to-become-colombias-president |access-date=8 September 2022 |work=The Guardian |date=17 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618005334/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/jun/18/ivan-duque-wins-election-to-become-colombias-president |archive-date=18 June 2018 |location=Bogotá}}</ref> The elections were the first to occur since congress passed legislation in 2015 modifying the presidential term limits, restricting presidents to one four-year term with a formal ban on re-election. The bill also granted the runner-up candidate in a presidential election a seat in the [[Senate of Colombia|senate]], whilst their running mate would become a member of the [[Chamber of Representatives of Colombia|chamber of representatives]].<ref name=crs>{{cite web |title=Colombia's 2018 elections |url=https://sgp.fas.org/crs/row/IF10817.pdf |publisher=Congressional Research Service |access-date=8 September 2022 |date=12 July 2018}}</ref>   

Duque, a lawyer and protege of former president [[Álvaro Uribe]], was the nominee for the [[Democratic Center (Colombia)|Democratic Center]] party. Duque, who was mostly unknown to the public before the election,<ref name=duque/> had a platform encompassing support for innovation, economic recovery and staunch opposition to the peace agreement.<ref name=crs/> 

Petro, a former [[19th of April Movement|AD/M-19]] member and nominee for the [[Humane Colombia]] party, ran a progressive populist campaign that focused on reducing inequality and implementing universal healthcare and environmental protection. Petro narrowly surpassed centrist candidate Sergio Fajardo in the first round by just over 1%, permitting him to qualify for the run-off.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Isabel Perñaranda |title=Gustavo Petro's Dream |url=https://jacobin.com/2018/06/colombia-elections-populism-uribe-duque-petro |access-date=8 September 2022 |work=Jacobin |date=17 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220908090703/https://jacobin.com/2018/06/colombia-elections-populism-uribe-duque-petro |archive-date=8 September 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> 

Duque was victorious in the second round, earning 54% of the vote to Petro's 41%.<ref name="jpd"/> Duque won 24 of Colombia's 32 departments, performed well in the inland regions and received over 77% of the vote in the Andean department of [[Norte de Santander Department|Norte de Santander]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Presidential Election of May-June 2018 |url=http://psephos.adam-carr.net/countries/c/colombia/colombiapres20182.txt |publisher=Psephos |access-date=8 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191125183600/http://psephos.adam-carr.net/countries/c/colombia/colombiapres20182.txt |archive-date=25 November 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Duque assumed office on 7 August.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-45107063|title=Iván Duque: Colombia's new president sworn into office|date=8 September 2018|newspaper=BBC News World Latin America|access-date=8 August 2018|archive-date=30 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201030105110/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-45107063|url-status=live}}</ref> Meanwhile, Petro became a senator, and later the future president of Colombia, whilst his running mate, Ángela Robledo, became a member of the chamber of representatives.<ref>{{cite news |title=Meet Gustavo Petro, Colombian Former Guerrilla & Leftist Who Mounted Historic Campaign for Presidency |url=https://www.democracynow.org/2018/8/10/meet_gustavo_petro_colombian_former_guerilla |access-date=8 September 2022 |work=Democracy Now |date=10 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422060718/https://www.democracynow.org/2018/8/10/meet_gustavo_petro_colombian_former_guerilla |archive-date=22 April 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Corte Constitucional tumbó la curul de Ángela María Robledo |url=https://www.lafm.com.co/politica/atencion-corte-constitucional-tumbo-la-curul-de-angela-maria-robledo |access-date=8 September 2022 |work=La FM |date=1 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210711013802/https://www.lafm.com.co/politica/atencion-corte-constitucional-tumbo-la-curul-de-angela-maria-robledo |archive-date=11 July 2021|url-status=live|language=es}}</ref>

== Background ==
{{Further|2014 Colombian presidential election|2016 Colombian peace agreement referendum|Colombian peace process}}
During the previous election held in 2014, a second round was held as no candidate attained more than 50% of the vote. The candidates were incumbent president Juan Manuel Santos of the [[Union Party for the People|Party of the U]] and [[Óscar Iván Zuluaga]] of the [[Democratic Center (Colombia)|Democratic Center]]. The election centred on the issue of how to continue peace negotiations with the [[Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia]] (FARC).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=Richard |title=Colombia Presidential Elections 2014: Unpacking the Complexities of Peace |url=https://www.panoramas.pitt.edu/news-and-politics/colombia-presidential-elections-2014-unpacking-complexities-peace |access-date=29 August 2022 |work=Panoramas |date=13 October 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210624050659/https://www.panoramas.pitt.edu/news-and-politics/colombia-presidential-elections-2014-unpacking-complexities-peace |archive-date=24 June 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> In the run-off, Santos defeated Zuluaga, earning 50% of the vote to his opponent's 44%.<ref>{{cite web |title=Elección de Presidente y Vicepresidente - 2ª Vuelta |url=https://elecciones.registraduria.gov.co:81/elecciones2014/presidente/2v/99PR2/DPR9999999_L1.htm |publisher=CNE |access-date=29 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220602034608/https://elecciones.registraduria.gov.co:81/elecciones2014/presidente/2v/99PR2/DPR9999999_L1.htm |archive-date=2 June 2022 |language=es |url-status=live}}</ref>

In June 2015, both houses of congress approved legislation limiting presidents to a single four-year term with a formal ban on re-election, restoring the term limits that were in place before 2005. President Santos, who submitted the bill upon re-election in the 2014 presidential election, said the re-election ban would restore a balance of power in Colombia. The bill faced criticism from supporters and members of the [[Democratic Center (Colombia)|Democratic Center]] party led by former president and rival of Santos, [[Álvaro Uribe]]. They argued the purpose of the bill's introduction was to prevent Uribe from seeking a non-consecutive third term to the presidency. Edward Rodríguez, a Democratic Center member of congress, described the re-election ban as "revenge against Uribe".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Reyes |first1=Elizabeth |title=Colombian lawmakers approve a one-term limit for presidents |url=https://english.elpais.com/elpais/2015/06/04/inenglish/1433416990_898964.html |access-date=1 September 2022 |work=El País |date=4 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200922023726/https://english.elpais.com/elpais/2015/06/04/inenglish/1433416990_898964.html |archive-date=22 September 2020|url-status=live|location=Bogotá}}</ref>

On 27 September 2016, the government signed a peace agreement with the FARC. A referendum took place on 12 October to ratify the deal; however, voters narrowly rejected it. President Santos then negotiated with staunch opponents of the agreement like Uribe and reentered into talks with the FARC. The FARC and the Colombian government agreed to a revised peace deal on 24 November. The modified agreement included changes, such as an agreement that the deal would not be part of the constitution. Rather than put the revised deal through another referendum for ratification, Santos sent the agreement to congress.<ref>{{cite news |title=Colombia signs new peace deal with Farc |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-38096179 |access-date=29 August 2022 |work=BBC |date=24 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220827234130/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-38096179 |archive-date=27 August 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> The Senate voted to ratify the deal on 29 November, which was followed by the approval by the chamber of representatives. The ratification marked an end to 50 years of conflict.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Partlow |first1=Joshua |last2=Miroff |first2=Nick |title=Colombia's congress approves historic peace deal with FARC rebels |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/the_americas/colombian-congress-approves-historic-peace-deal/2016/11/30/9b2fda92-b5a7-11e6-939c-91749443c5e5_story.html |access-date=29 August 2022 |newspaper=Washington Post |date=30 November 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201090410/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/the_americas/colombian-congress-approves-historic-peace-deal/2016/11/30/9b2fda92-b5a7-11e6-939c-91749443c5e5_story.html |archive-date=1 December 2016|url-status=live |location=Mexico City}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Colombian Presidents are elected for four-year terms using a two-round system; if no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, a runoff is held between the top two candidates.<ref name=ASCOA/> The Vice President is elected on the same ticket as the President. Presidents are limited to a single four-year term and Article 191 of the [[Colombian Constitution of 1991|constitution]] requires candidates to be Colombian by birth and at least thirty years old.  

In line with the constitution, Colombian citizens by birth or by naturalization, aged eighteen or older have the right to vote. Several scenarios can cause the loss of the right to vote, as outlined in the constitution. Citizens in detention centers can vote from the establishments determined by the [[National Civil Registry (Colombia)|National Civil Registry]]. The civil registry inscription is not automatic, and citizens must go to the regional office of the Registry to register.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.colombia.com/especiales/elecciones_2007/preguntas/preguntas3.asp |title= ¿Cómo funciona el proceso de inscripción de cédulas?|publisher= Colombia |author= Colombia}}</ref>

Legislative act # 2 of 2015 established that the runner-up in the presidential elections is given a seat in the [[Senate of Colombia|Senate]] and their Vice President candidate will become a member of the [[House of Representatives (Colombia)|House of Representatives]].<ref name="ActoLegislativo02/2015">{{cite news |url=http://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/acto_legislativo_02_2015.html|title=Acto Legislativo número 02 de 2015 |access-date=5 March 2018 |website= Secretaria General del Senado |language=es|ref=El candidato que le siga en votos a quien la autoridad electoral declare elegido en el cargo de Presidente y Vicepresidente de la República, Gobernador de Departamento, Alcalde Distrital y Alcalde municipal tendrá el derecho personal a ocupar una curul en el Senado, Cámara de Representantes, Asamblea Departamental, Concejo Distrital y Concejo Municipal, respectivamente, durante el período de la correspondiente corporación.}}</ref>

