===Candidates that withdrew===
The [[Common Alternative Revolutionary Force]] withdrew its presidential aspiration on 9 March 2018, stating the health problems of its candidate [[Timoleón Jiménez|Rodrigo Londoño]], as the reason.<ref>{{cite news|last=Manetto|first=Francesco|title=Las FARC retiran su candidatura a la presidencia de Colombia|url=https://elpais.com/internacional/2018/03/08/colombia/1520516996_090050.html|date=9 March 2018|access-date=31 May 2018|work=El País|issn=1134-6582|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220901052024/https://elpais.com/internacional/2018/03/08/colombia/1520516996_090050.html|archive-date=1 September 2022|url-status=live| language=es}}</ref>

Juan Carlos Pinzón, who had previously served as defence minister and ambassador to the United States under president Juan Manuel Santos, launched his candidacy for [[Casa de Nariño]] in 2017. Pinzón withdrew from the race on 16 March 2018 to become Vargas Lleras' running mate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Vargas Lleras confirma a Juan Carlos Pinzón como fórmula vicepresidencial |url=https://www.elespectador.com/elecciones-2018/noticias/politica/vargas-lleras-confirma-juan-carlos-pinzon-como-formula-vicepresidencial-articulo-744769 |access-date=31 March 2018 |work=El Espectador |date=16 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180316171857/https://www.elespectador.com/elecciones-2018/noticias/politica/vargas-lleras-confirma-juan-carlos-pinzon-como-formula-vicepresidencial-articulo-744769 |archive-date=16 March 2018|url-status=live |language=es-CO}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=JUAN CARLOS PINZÓN BUENO |date=12 February 2021 |url=https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/juan-carlos-pinzon-bueno |publisher=La Silla Vacía |access-date=27 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220309082848/https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/juan-carlos-pinzon-bueno |archive-date=9 March 2022 |language=es |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Piedad Córdoba]] and her vice presidential candidate [[:es:Jaime Araújo Rentería|Jaime Araújo Rentería]] terminated their candidacy on 9 April 2018.<ref>{{cite news|last=Bonces|first=Eduardo|title=Piedad Córdoba no despegó y se retiró|url=http://www.elcolombiano.com/elecciones-2018-colombia/piedad-cordoba-no-despego-y-se-retiro-IF8520479|access-date=10 April 2018|date=10 April 2018|work=El Colombiano|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220606221613/http://www.elcolombiano.com/elecciones-2018-colombia/piedad-cordoba-no-despego-y-se-retiro-IF8520479|archive-date=6 June 2022|url-status=live| language=es-ES}}</ref>

[[Viviane Morales Hoyos|Viviane Morales]] and her vice presidential candidate Jorge Leyva Durán, of the ''Somos Región Colombia'' party, retired their candidacy on 2 May 2018.<ref>{{cite news|last=Muñoz|first=Leonardo|title=Viviane Morales Renuncia a su candidatura presidencial|url=http://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-colombia-2018/presidenciales/viviane-morales-renuncia-a-candidatura-a-la-presidencia-212464|access-date=2 May 2018|work=El Tiempo|language=es-ES}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Viviane Morales renunció a su candidatura presidencial|url=https://www.lafm.com.co/politica/viviane-morales-renuncio-su-candidatura-presidencial/|access-date=2 May 2018|work=LAFM|language=es-ES}}</ref> After, Morales adhered to Iván Duque's campaign, while Leyva joined Germán Vargas Lleras' candidacy.<ref>{{cite news|last=Marulanda|first=Olga Patricia Rendón|title=Partido de Viviane Morales se dividió entre Duque y Vargas Lleras|url=http://www.elcolombiano.com/elecciones-2018-colombia/partido-de-viviane-morales-adhirio-a-german-vargas-lleras-LD8665683|access-date=31 May 2018|work=El Colombiano|language=es-ES}}</ref>

== Primaries ==

Two coalitions, the right-wing Great Alliance for Colombia and left-wing Social Inclusion for Peace, held primary elections concurrently with the legislative elections on 11 March.<ref name=eprs/>

=== Great Alliance for Colombia ===
Three candidates sought the nomination of the conservative Great Alliance for Colombia:<ref name=cne>{{cite web |title=CONSULTAS PRESIDENCIALS |url=https://www.registraduria.gov.co/?page=E2018_ConsultasPresidenciales |publisher=CNE |access-date=18 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180222044842/https://www.registraduria.gov.co/?page=E2018_ConsultasPresidenciales |archive-date=22 February 2018 |language=es |date=18 January 2018 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

* Iván Duque of the Democratic Center, a young lawyer who had served in the senate since 2014.<ref name=aq>{{cite web |title=Meet the Candidates: Colombia |url=https://www.americasquarterly.org/fulltextarticle/meet-the-candidates-colombia/ |publisher=Americas Quarterly |access-date=18 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201203110202/https://www.americasquarterly.org/fulltextarticle/meet-the-candidates-colombia/ |archive-date=3 December 2020 |date=17 January 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

* [[Marta Lucía Ramírez]], formerly a Conservative party member, sought the nomination as an independent. Ramírez served as defence minister to Álvaro Uribe from 2002 to 2003 and was the first woman to hold that position. She had most recently served as a senator.<ref name=aq/>

* Alejandro Ordóñez, a former inspector-general who competed as a member of the La Patria de Pie party.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alejandro Ordóñez |date=12 February 2021 |url=https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/alejandro-ordonez |publisher=La Silla Vacía |access-date=18 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210802022306/https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/alejandro-ordonez |archive-date=2 August 2021|url-status=live|language=es}}</ref><ref name=cne/>

