Petro secured the nomination by a large margin and won all departments, losing only in Caceido's home department of [[Magdalena Department|Magdalena]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Gustavo Petro ganó en la consulta Inclusión Social por la Paz con 2,83 millones de votos |url=https://www.larepublica.co/economia/gustavo-petro-gano-en-la-consulta-inclusion-social-por-la-paz-con-261-millones-de-votos-2609210 |access-date=18 September 2022 |work=La República |date=11 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901055146/https://www.larepublica.co/economia/gustavo-petro-gano-en-la-consulta-inclusion-social-por-la-paz-con-261-millones-de-votos-2609210 |archive-date=1 September 2022 |language=es}}</ref> Following the primary election, Petro selected [[Ángela Robledo]] of the Green Alliance and a member of the chamber of representatives as his running mate.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ángela María Robledo |date=12 February 2021 |url=https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/angela-maria-robledo |publisher=La Silla Vacía |access-date=18 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210920232437/https://www.lasillavacia.com/quien-es-quien/angela-maria-robledo |archive-date=20 September 2021|url-status=live |language=es}}</ref>

== Campaign ==

According to polling data, significant issues amongst voters were unemployment, healthcare, corruption and the FARC peace agreement.<ref name=ASCOA/>

[[Iván Duque]], a lawyer, senator, protege of Uribe, and the nominee for the Democratic Center party, campaigned on promoting innovation, job creation, economic recovery, environmental preservation and opposing the FARC peace agreement. Before his nomination, most of the public was not familiar with Duque.<ref name=duque/> He rose to prominence during the 2016 peace referendum due to his staunch opposition. However, he stated that Duque was still in favour of peace but aimed to keep the FARC out of Colombian politics and prosecute FARC and [[National Liberation Army (Colombia)|National Liberation Army]] (ELN) rebels. On narcotics, Duque expressed his support for the war on drugs and sought to eliminate all coca production. He emphasised a message of opposing corruption and implementing economic reform; for instance; he proposed a simplification of the tax code. Due to Duque's youth and light experience, opponents and critics painted him as unprepared to head the executive branch, leading some to claim further that the senator was a puppet of Uribe. Duque countered these claims by saying it was time for a new generation to take over, and whilst he did highlight his close ties to Uribe, he emphasised that he was "his own man". Critics also attacked Duque for his opposition to the peace agreement, his refusal to rule out [[Hydraulic fracturing|fracking]] and alleged ties to [[Right-wing paramilitarism in Colombia|paramilitary groups]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Presidential candidates: Iván Duque |url=https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-ivan-duque/29989/ |access-date=3 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=23 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530065611/https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-ivan-duque/29989/ |archive-date=30 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Di Salvo |first1=Matthew |title=Ivan Duque chosen as Centro Democratico's presidential candidate as other endorsements begin to shape 2018 election in Colombia |url=https://thebogotapost.com/ivan-duque-chosen-centro-democraticos-presidential-candidate-endorsements-begin-shape-2018-election-colombia/26118/ |access-date=3 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=13 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210924100719/https://thebogotapost.com/ivan-duque-chosen-centro-democraticos-presidential-candidate-endorsements-begin-shape-2018-election-colombia/26118/ |archive-date=24 September 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

Economist [[Gustavo Petro]] of Humane Colombia, a former mayor of Bogotá and former [[19th of April Movement|M-19 guerilla]] who was previously a candidate in the [[2010 Colombian presidential election|2010 presidential election]], ran a populist campaign focusing on decreasing inequality and tackling [[climate change]]. Supported by a coalition of left and centre-left parties, Petro also emphasised workers' rights and campaigned to improve labour conditions in both urban and rural areas. He aimed to reduce poverty by implementing land reforms and establishing a "popular bank" that would offer loans to impoverished citizens. Petro supported the introduction of state-funded universal healthcare that would replace the unpopular private-funded system. Furthermore, he sought to restrict the use of fossil fuels and intended to ban any further exploration of mining and fracking in favour of renewable energy sources. Several months before the election, Petro survived an assassination attempt when a bullet pierced through his armoured van. He presented himself as a successor to prominent slain left-wing politicians such as [[Jorge Eliecer Gaitan]] and [[Luis Carlos Galán]]. Petro furthermore highlighted the need to end political persecution in the country. Petro's opponents claimed that he would "turn Colombia into another Venezuela", citing his prior support for former Venezuelan president [[Hugo Chávez]]. Critics said his policy proposals would not attract foreign investment and that his plans to abandon non-renewable energy, which accounted for 2-4% of Colombia's [[Gross domestic product|GDP]], were not feasible at the time. Opponents also claimed that his policies would result in an economic disaster.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rampietti |first1=Alessandro |title=Colombia: Can leftist Gustavo Petro become president? |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2018/5/27/colombia-can-leftist-gustavo-petro-become-president |access-date=3 September 2022 |work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |date=27 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220712172411/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2018/5/27/colombia-can-leftist-gustavo-petro-become-president |archive-date=12 July 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Newbery |first1=Emma |last2=Pritchard |first2=Oli |last3=Huigen |first3=Freek |title=Presidential candidates: Gustavo Petro |url=https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-gustavo-petro/29994/ |access-date=3 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=22 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530065558/https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-gustavo-petro/29994/ |archive-date=30 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

Veteran politician [[Humberto De la Calle]], who served as vice president to [[Ernesto Samper]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Candidate Profile: Humberto de la Calle |url=https://colombiafocus.com/candidate-profile-humberto-de-la-calle |publisher=Colombia Focus |access-date=5 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210805140745/https://colombiafocus.com/candidate-profile-humberto-de-la-calle |archive-date=5 August 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref> was the nominee for the [[Colombian Liberal Party]]. De la Calle played a crucial role in negotiating the peace deal with the FARC and campaigned on policies to assist the country post-conflict, including investments in job creation and regions that were heavily affected by the conflict. He entered the presidential race to keep Duque from winning the presidency. De la Calle supported increasing environmental protection and stricter criteria for the issue of mining licenses, both as a means of promoting ecotourism. Due to the high presence of labour inequality and gender-based violence, De la Calle announced his intention to establish a ministry of women to tackle these issues. He furthermore proposed a significant reform of the education sector, seeking to transform the financing and structure and change the ministry of education to the ministry of intelligence. De la Calle's proposals included other policies with broad support amongst the youth, such as modifying the unpopular student loan program and abolishing compulsory military service. Whilst on the campaign trail, De la Calle struggled to gain momentum and often trailed his opponents in the polls. The peace agreement, a controversial topic amongst voters, did not help boost support for De la Calle. His 30 years of political experience proved a double-edged sword; some voters saw this as a benefit, but others concluded that De la Calle was a part of the elite. The Liberal party's severe dis-unity and rupturing also hurt him. But De la Calle's campaign suffered a severe downturn when Liberal party leader and former president [[César Gaviria]] announced before the first round that the Liberal party would support Duque in the run-off, implying that the party had lost confidence in De la Calle's candidacy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Newbury |first1=Emma |last2=Pritchard |first2=Oli |last3=Huigen |first3=Freek |title=Presidential candidates: Humberto de la Calle |url=https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-humberto-de-la-calle/29987/ |access-date=5 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=22 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530071142/https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-humberto-de-la-calle/29987/ |archive-date=30 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Acosta |first1=Luis Jaime |title=Colombia's De La Calle seeks to protect peace deal if wins presidency |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-colombia-election-delacalle/colombias-de-la-calle-seeks-to-protect-peace-deal-if-wins-presidency-idUSKBN1HX2UY |access-date=5 September 2022 |work=Reuters |date=27 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220321065325/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-colombia-election-delacalle/colombias-de-la-calle-seeks-to-protect-peace-deal-if-wins-presidency-idUSKBN1HX2UY |archive-date=21 March 2022 |url-status=live|location=[[Bogotá]]}}</ref>

[[Sergio Fajardo]], a mathematician, former mayor of Medellín, former governor of [[Antioquia department|Antioquia]] and previously a vice presidential candidate in the [[2010 Colombian presidential election|2010 presidential election]], won the nomination of the Colombia Coalition, an alliance composed of the [[Alternative Democratic Pole]], the [[Green Alliance (Colombia)|Green Alliance]] and his Civic Compromise party. Fajardo's platform encompassed education and the environment. He intended to increase the education budget by 10% and boost enrollment nationwide to combat poverty. Fajardo supported the peace agreement, although he presented himself as a moderate centrist alternative to Duque and Petro. On the campaign trail, Fajardo expressed concern about vote buying and advocated for a transparent electoral and judicial process. Whilst he campaigned on being the fiscally reliable candidate, he faced criticism from opponents who highlighted a dramatic debt increase that occurred during his tenure as governor of Antioquia. Fajardo considered polarisation a threat to Colombian politics and sought to present himself as a middle-ground candidate. However, critics said this strategy risked Fajardo appealing to few voters.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sergio Fajardo |url=https://especiales.semana.com/elecciones-presidenciales-2018/candidatos-hoja-de-vida/sergio-fajardo.html |publisher=Semana |access-date=7 September 2022 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20220906002414/https://especiales.semana.com/elecciones-presidenciales-2018/candidatos-hoja-de-vida/sergio-fajardo.html |archive-date=6 September 2022 |language=es |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Newbery |first1=Emma |last2=Pritchard |first2=Oli |last3=Huigen |first3=Freek |title=Presidential candidates: Sergio Fajardo |url=https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-sergio-fajardo/29991/ |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=24 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200921004825/https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-sergio-fajardo/29991/ |archive-date=21 September 2020|url-status=live}}</ref>

Jorge Antonio Trujillo, a Christian pastor and former senator, was the nominee for the We Are All Colombia party. Trujillo, the founder of the gospel church of Casa de Reino, gained widespread attention in 2014 when he claimed that the winter wave the country experienced was a punishment from God and blamed President Santos, claiming that the president had previously travelled to the [[Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta]] mountains where he made a "secret rite" and "handed over the country to the shamans and sorcerers." Whilst campaigning, Trujillo frequently mentioned that his most fundamental priorities were in the following order: God, family, the Church and the nation.<ref>{{cite news |title=¿Quién es Jorge Antonio Trujillo, el candidato desconocido en el tarjetón? |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/quien-es-jorge-antonio-trujillo-el-candidato-desconocido-en-el-tarjeton-article-745847/ |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=El Espectador |date=22 March 2018 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20220905111141/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/quien-es-jorge-antonio-trujillo-el-candidato-desconocido-en-el-tarjeton-article-745847/ |archive-date=5 September 2022|url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> An opponent of [[LGBT rights in Colombia|LGBT rights]], Trujillo pledged to prevent LGBT couples from legally adopting children and outlaw same-sex marriage. Trujillo was opposed to the peace agreement, claiming it violated the constitution. Rarely holding public rallies, he instead preached his campaign messages through his church services. Trujillo performed poorly in opinion polls, although the pastor claimed to have faced discrimination from the national electoral council and pollsters. However, Trujillo assured voters he had faith that God would provide an "electoral surprise" in the first round and qualify for the run-off.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Oscar Iván Rey |title=Jorge Trujillo, el candidato con la fe de llegar a segunda vuelta |url=https://www.vanguardia.com/politica/jorge-trujillo-el-candidato-con-la-fe-de-llegar-a-segunda-vuelta-PEVL433826 |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=Vanguardia |date=23 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220906073038/https://www.vanguardia.com/politica/jorge-trujillo-el-candidato-con-la-fe-de-llegar-a-segunda-vuelta-PEVL433826 |archive-date=6 September 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref>

[[Germán Vargas Lleras]] of the conservative [[Radical Change]] party and vice president to Juan Manuel Santos resigned as second-in-command in March 2017 to be eligible to contest the presidency.<ref>{{cite news |title=Senado aprobó la renuncia del vicepresidente Germán Vargas |url=https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/senado-aprobo-la-renuncia-del-vicepresidente-german-vargas-2486561 |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=Asuntos Legales |date=21 March 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201026033953/https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/senado-aprobo-la-renuncia-del-vicepresidente-german-vargas-2486561 |archive-date=26 October 2020|url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> Vargas Lleras ran as an independent; at a time when various members of his party were under investigation for corruption. Vargas Lleras led in many early polls and with the support of the Mejor Vargas Lleras coalition; the former vice
president campaigned on his experience, pledging to continue many policies of the Santos administration, for example, resuming the 4G road network infrastructure that he played a part in initiating. On the economy, the former vice president supported lowering income taxes to attract more foreign investment and promote entrepreneurs, differing from the policy of the Santos administration.<ref name=lleras>{{cite news |last1=Newbery |first1=Emma |last2=Pritchard |first2=Oli |last3=Huigen |first3=Freek |title=Presidential candidates: Germán Vargas Lleras |url=https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-german-vargas-lleras/29996/ |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=The Bogotá Post |date=22 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530071103/https://thebogotapost.com/presidential-candidates-german-vargas-lleras/29996/ |archive-date=30 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> Furthermore, Vargas Lleras highlighted the [[Crisis in Venezuela]] during the campaign and expressed staunch opposition towards the [[Nicolás Maduro]]. On the campaign trail, Vargas Lleras claimed he was the candidate that would prevent Colombia from descending into a crisis like in Venezuela.<ref>{{cite news |title=Venezuela, el caballo de batalla del comité de firmas de Vargas Lleras |url=https://www.semana.com/nacion/articulo/venezuela-en-la-campana-del-comite-de-firmas-de-german-vargas-lleras/538098/ |access-date=7 September 2022 |work=Semana |date=29 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220317213904/https://www.semana.com/nacion/articulo/venezuela-en-la-campana-del-comite-de-firmas-de-german-vargas-lleras/538098/ |archive-date=17 March 2022|url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> The former vice president advocated for an end to corruption; however, this was complicated when opponents attacked Vargas Lleras for the corruption investigations surrounding the Radical Change party. Critics said his tax reform proposal would risk increasing inequality throughout Colombia and that the support from president Santos would prove to be a double-edged sword due to the outgoing administration's unpopularity. Furthermore, Vargas Lleras did not fully support the FARC peace agreement.<ref name=lleras/>

== Debates ==
There were four debates, which took place in the four main departments. 

The Antioquia debate was characterised by the lack of attacks made by the candidates. Topics included public order, Venezuelan immigration, the peace treaties with FARC and LGBT couples' rights.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.semana.com/elecciones-presidenciales-2018/noticias/debate-presidencial-en-antioquia-sin-agravios-pero-con-pullas-562406 |title=Debate presidencial en Antioquia: Sin agravios pero con pullas |website=Semana |access-date=12 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420112837/http://www.semana.com/elecciones-presidenciales-2018/noticias/debate-presidencial-en-antioquia-sin-agravios-pero-con-pullas-562406 |archive-date=20 April 2018 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Regional topics included the Antioquia railroad, regional connectivity and tourist industry development.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bonces|first=Eduardo|date=2018-04-03|title=Candidatos hablaron sobre Ferrocarril de Antioquia|url=https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/candidatos-en-el-primer-debate-XL8490202|access-date=2020-09-28|website=El Colombiano|language=es-ES}}</ref>

The Caribe debate saw several disqualifications and confrontations between candidates, as well as incidents in the crowds between followers of different parties.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Debate Caribe|url=https://www.elheraldo.co/columnas-de-opinion/debate-caribe-479278|access-date=2020-09-28|website=El Heraldo|date=7 April 2018 |language=es}}</ref> Topics included the battle against corruption, the peace process with FARC, the environment and the country's relationship with Venezuelan.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Los momentos más álgidos del debate presidencial en el Caribe|url=https://www.elpais.com.co/elecciones/elecciones-presidenciales/los-momentos-mas-algidos-del-debate-presidencial-en-el-caribe-1.html|access-date=2020-09-28|website=El País}}</ref> Regional topics included Electricaribe (an energy supplier in the Caribe region) and the decisions that the candidates would take to resolve the energy problem.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2018-04-20|title=Debate Caribe: Lo que dijeron los candidatos a la Presidencia sobre Electricaribe |work=El Heraldo|url=https://www.elheraldo.co/politica/debate-caribe-lo-que-dijeron-los-candidatos-la-presidencia-sobre-electricaribe-478656|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420113309/https://www.elheraldo.co/politica/debate-caribe-lo-que-dijeron-los-candidatos-la-presidencia-sobre-electricaribe-478656|archive-date=2018-04-20|access-date=2020-09-28}}</ref>

The Pacific debate saw discussions on flaws in the healthcare system, illegal mining, education, FARC, illegal crops, the battle against corruption, infrastructure, culture, racism and the exploitation of biodiversity. The candidates Humberto de La Calle and Germán Vargas Lleras took advantage of the free questions to comment on absent candidate, Iván Duque.<ref>{{Cite web|title=¿Qué proponen los candidatos para el Pacífico?, esto dijeron en el debate|url=https://www.elpais.com.co/elecciones/elecciones-presidenciales/que-proponen-los-candidatos-para-el-pacifico-esto-dijeron-en-el-debate.html|access-date=2020-09-28|website=El País}}</ref>

The Eje Cafetero debate was cancelled due to disturbances that prevented some candidates entering the  Los Fundadores de Manizales theatre. Afterwards Iván Duque, Humberto de La Calle, Gustavo Petro and Germán Vargas Lleras met at the city's auditorium and held a closed-door debate. The most important topics were the coffee industry and tourism.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2018-04-18|title=Café y Turismo, temas clave del debate a puerta cerrada en Manizales|url=https://www.eltiempo.com/colombia/otras-ciudades/debate-en-manizales-se-hizo-a-puerta-cerrada-por-falta-de-garantias-en-seguridad-206714|access-date=2020-09-28|website=El Tiempo|language=es}}</ref>

==Opinion polls==
===First round===
====2017====
During the first round, six parties ran for the presidency. These candidates were; [[Iván Duque]], [[Gustavo Petro]], [[Sergio Fajardo]], [[Germán Vargas Lleras]], [[Humberto De la Calle]] and Jorge Antonio Trujillo. As none of the candidates received the majority of votes, the elections moved to a runoff between the top two candidates, Iván Duque and Gustavo Petro, which was held on 17 June 2018.

